window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('e1',{"s":["<section id=\"e1-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · 2017–2026</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Generating diverse partners to train robust teammates</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>Ad hoc teamwork</b> · Stone et al. 2010: cooperate with unknown teammates with no prior coordination or shared protocol.</li><li><b>Self-play conventions</b> · Self-play agents invent private conventions; Other-Play (2020) needs known symmetries, OBL (2021) drops them.</li><li><b>Hand-made or random pools</b> · Pools came from experts, MAP-Elites (Canaan 2019) or FCP&#x27;s seeds and checkpoints (no diversity check).</li><li><b>Type-based agents</b> · PLASTIC (Barrett 2017) picks among hand-made teammate types, so unseen styles are missed.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Train one agent that cooperates with teammates it never met, using only simulated partners, when hand-made or random partner sets miss many strategies.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Generate the partners on purpose: reward diversity (trajectories, intent, low cross-play, regret) and train on them, or on a noisy self-copy or many tasks.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/e1_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Backbone only; solid arrows are inheritance or reuse; white pills are guests. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here.</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">Cross-play (XP)</b> · play between agents trained separately</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">Best response (BR)</b> · policy with top return against a fixed partner</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">Handshake</b> · cue that lets an agent spot cross-play, then sabotage</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">Ego agent</b> · the one learner trained on the partner set</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diverse partners and environments</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (6 guests) · 2017–2026 · OBL → LIPO → BRDiv → ROTATE</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Partner generation assumes a simulator and few agents</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#80511E\">Simulator for partners</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Every paper trains its partners and the learner on rollouts in a simulator; none learns from real interaction alone.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#80511E\">Two agents, shared reward</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Almost all tests are two-player cooperative games; only ROTATE adds a 4-agent foraging extension.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#80511E\">Distinct solutions exist</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Diversity needs several ways to cooperate (LIPO, BRDiv); Specialists also needs a hand-written play feature.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#80511E\">Fixed test partners</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Held-out partners are programmed or trained agents; people only in FCP (114), E3T (20), CEC, CoMeDi, UPD.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#80511E\">Gaps later papers relax</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Type-free AHT drops types; OBL drops symmetries; Adversity, CoMeDi cut sabotage; ROTATE adds competence; CEC varies tasks.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Ad hoc teamwork (Stone), PLASTIC types, Other-Play and OBL, FCP and E3T partner recipes, plus MAP-Elites Hanabi pools (Canaan).</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#80511E\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Partner sets that beat earlier sets on each paper&#x27;s own test; CEC and UPD add unseen tasks and humans. No shared benchmark.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diverse partners and environments</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (6 guests) · 2017–2026 · OBL → LIPO → BRDiv → ROTATE</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">From spreading policies apart to regret-driven partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">Each step patches a gap: look-alike partners, sabotage, missing best responses, weak teammates, few tasks; guests OBL and E3T supply parts.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/e1_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:560px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diverse partners and environments</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (6 guests) · 2017–2026 · OBL → LIPO → BRDiv → ROTATE</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Spread policies apart</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2021–2023</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Type-free AHT</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">TrajeDi</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Any-Play</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Hanabi meta-agent</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">Target entropy, trajectory divergence or decoded intent set the spread (FCP&#x27;s seed pool is the plain baseline); Canaan&#x27;s agent guesses type with Bayes.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#80511E\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Cross-play, best response</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2023–2024</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">LIPO</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Adversity</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CoMeDi</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">BRDiv</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">L-BRDiv</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Specialists</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">LIPO: low cross-play. Adversity (on OBL), CoMeDi (on Conventions): stop sabotage. BRDiv, L-BRDiv: distinct best responses. Specialists: distill.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#80511E\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Regret, language, tasks</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2025–2026</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">SemDiv</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">ROTATE</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CEC</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#F7EEE3;border:2px solid #80511E;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">UPD</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">SemDiv: LLM-written teammates. ROTATE: ego regret. CEC: many tasks. UPD: keeps learnable partners, replacing E3T&#x27;s fixed self-copy mix.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diverse partners and environments</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (6 guests) · 2017–2026 · OBL → LIPO → BRDiv → ROTATE</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · PLASTIC 2017 · 1 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: reuse the past teammate a new team resembles</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Inferring teammates tree; here as the type-based learner. Builds on ad hoc teamwork, UCT, FQI (outside). Type-free AHT compares against it; BRDiv uses it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Known environment; teammates stationary</li><li>New teammates resemble past or hand-coded types</li><li>Past teams available to learn from</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Tracks which past teammate type the new team resembles (loss-bounded belief) and acts on it: models plus UCT, or one policy per past team.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">BRDiv trains PLASTIC Policy as its learner; Type-free AHT only compares against it. No member builds on its method.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Pursuit, unseen student teams: 13.36 vs 9.97 prey caught for copying teammates (AIJ 2017).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Making friends on the fly: Cooperating with new teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Barrett et al. · AIJ 2017</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Other-Play 2020 · 2 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: relabeled partners break arbitrary conventions</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Symmetry and belief tree (zero-shot coordination); here as the source of the ZSC setting and OP agents. Builds on self-play and SAD (outside).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Game symmetries are known and given</li><li>Agents train separately and meet once</li><li>Partners are also trained for zero-shot play</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Trains each agent against a randomly relabeled copy of its partner, using the game&#x27;s symmetries, so only convention-free strategies survive.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">OBL adopts its setting; TrajeDi and Adversity train OP agents; Any-Play and E3T compare against it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi, 2 players: cross-play 2.52 to 15.32 (SAD vs SAD+OP, max 25); 22.07 with SAD+AUX+OP (Table 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">“Other-Play” for Zero-Shot Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hu et al. · ICML 2020</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Type-free AHT 2021 · 3 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Generate teammates by target entropy; no types needed</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Compares against PLASTIC (guest, Inferring teammates tree) and AATEAM (outside); reuses DRQN and max-entropy RL. No member inherits from it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Self-play access in the task; no teammate types</li><li>Shared reward, partial views, two-agent teams</li><li>The unknown teammate&#x27;s strategy cannot be changed</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">EDRQN draws a target policy entropy each episode and feeds it to Q and alpha networks, so one self-play model yields many styles.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Cited as entropy-based generation by BRDiv, L-BRDiv, ROTATE; none inherits from it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">HFO, 5 teams outside the type set: 77.3% scoring vs AATEAM 73.2% and PLASTIC 70.3% (Table 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Learning with Generated Teammates to Achieve Type-Free Ad-Hoc Teamwork</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Xing et al. · IJCAI 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · OBL 2021 · 4 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: act optimally with no shared conventions</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Symmetry and belief tree; here as the belief model behind Adversity. Adopts Other-Play&#x27;s ZSC setting; builds on cognitive hierarchies and R2D2 (outside).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Turn-based cooperative Dec-POMDP</li><li>Known base policy pi0; learned belief model</li><li>Steppable simulator (notes&#x27; reading)</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reads the past as a fixed policy pi0 but plans the future as its own pi1, via a learned belief model; iterating gives levels 1 to 4.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Adversity builds on it (fictitious transitions, belief hierarchy); ROTATE reuses its recurrent belief model; Any-Play compares.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi, 2 players: cross-play 23.76 at level 4 vs 21.77 for Other-Play (max 25, Table 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Off-Belief Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hu et al. · ICML 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Conventions 2021 · 5 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: split rules from conventions to adapt fast</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Symmetry and belief tree; here as the source of CoMeDi&#x27;s convention-aware agent. Builds on Lewis&#x27;s convention concept and modular policy nets (outside).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Rational partners with known identities</li><li>Same domain; new tasks change the reward</li><li>Partners hand-designed or self-play trained</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A shared task module times one partner module per partner; a regularizer pulls the task module toward the marginal best response.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CoMeDi reuses its multitask routines to train a convention-aware agent from a diverse policy set (shared authors Shih, Sadigh).</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Arms 3 bandit: task module 0.01 from the true marginals vs 1.15 for BaselineAgg (Blocks 0.10 vs 0.44).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">On the Critical Role of Conventions in Adaptive Human-AI Collaboration</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Shih et al. · ICLR 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s06\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · TrajeDi 2021 · 6 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Train a best response to a deliberately diverse pool</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses Other-Play (guest): its Hanabi policies are OP agents plus an auxiliary task. Builds on Jensen-Shannon divergence and PBT. No member inherits it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player cooperative Dec-POMDP</li><li>Independent training, scored in cross-play</li><li>Pools trainable together (48-72 h per agent on Hanabi)</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A trajectory-level Jensen-Shannon diversity bonus on a self-play pool, plus one common best response trained against every member.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s06.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:338px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Baseline for LIPO, BRDiv, SemDiv and E3T; LIPO and L-BRDiv say trajectory differences need not be meaningful.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi (2 players): cross-play 24.09 vs 23.65 for Other-Play agents; a small gain from few seeds.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Trajectory Diversity for Zero-Shot Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Lupu et al. · ICML 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s07\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · FCP 2021 · 7 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: best response to a pool of seeds and checkpoints</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree; here as the pool recipe later papers reuse or beat. Builds on fictitious self-play and Carroll et al.&#x27;s Overcooked (outside).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Known shared reward; no human data</li><li>Zero-shot partners in a two-player kitchen</li><li>Pool chosen by hand (authors name this limit)</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Trains 32 self-play agents, keeps 3 checkpoints each, then one agent as the best response to the frozen pool.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s07.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:338px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Specialists reuses it for generalists; Any-Play, SemDiv, CEC, ROTATE, UPD and E3T compare against it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 5 kitchens: 10.6 vs 4.7 deliveries (FCP vs FCP-T, human proxy); beats SP, PP, BCP with 114 humans.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Collaborating with Humans without Human Data</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Strouse et al. · NeurIPS 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s08\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Any-Play 2022 · 8 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A diversity bonus helps agents cooperate with strangers</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Adds a DIAYN-style intent reward to SAD agents (outside). Compares against Other-Play and OBL agents (guests); discusses FCP and TrajeDi, runs neither.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>2-player Dec-POMDP, shared reward, no humans</li><li>No game symmetries given</li><li>Intent count N and weight lambda tuned</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A random intent steers a specializer; its partner (the accommodator) is rewarded for decoding it, so it handles many play-styles.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s08.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">BRDiv reimplements it as a baseline; LIPO cites its mutual-information idea. No member inherits from it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">2-player Hanabi, 128 models: inter-algorithm cross-play 14.2 vs 13.1 for the best baseline (Table 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Any-Play: An Intrinsic Augmentation for Zero-Shot Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Lucas et al. · AAMAS 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s09\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Hanabi meta-agent 2023 · 9 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guess the partner&#x27;s type, then play the best response</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends the authors&#x27; MAP-Elites Hanabi pools (CoG 2019) and ad hoc play. No member is its parent; Any-Play, LIPO, Specialists only cite it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Partner is one of a known pool, fixed for 10 games</li><li>Two behavior numbers reveal the type; offline pre-training</li><li>2-player Hanabi, rule-based partners</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A Bayes belief over partner types from two behavior features; it plays the response with the best belief-weighted score.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s09.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Cited by Any-Play, LIPO, Specialists as domain-specific pool building; nothing builds on it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">2-player Hanabi, same-pool tests: Adaptive 12.23 vs Generalist 11.90 of 25 (Pop. 2); no gain on unseen pools.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Generating and Adapting to Diverse Ad-Hoc Partners in Hanabi</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Canaan et al. · IEEE ToG 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s10\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · CoMeDi 2023 · 10 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Cross-play plus mixed-play yields diverse conventions</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses LIPO&#x27;s cross-play minimization (its baseline) and trains its agent with Conventions&#x27; multitask routines (guest); adds mixed play. Uses MAPPO, QDagger.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Fully observable, shared-reward games</li><li>Roll-outs only; no human data</li><li>Pool size fixed up front (8); alpha, beta tuned</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Train each new convention for high self-play but low cross-play reward with the most compatible earlier one, plus mixed-play so it cannot sabotage.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s10.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ROTATE reuses its policy-switching data; Specialists analyzes its mixed play (MP-reg); both also baseline it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked user study (25 users), Coordination Ring: score 3.68 vs 2.08 self-play and 3.25 expert human.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diverse Conventions for Human-AI Collaboration</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Sarkar et al. · NeurIPS 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s11\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Adversity 2023 · 11 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Flip a coin each turn so the partner cannot sabotage</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on OBL&#x27;s fictitious transitions and belief hierarchy (guest) and LIPO&#x27;s low-cross-play goal (SPWR is a simpler LIPO); uses Other-Play&#x27;s cross-play metric.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Turn-based play with public actions</li><li>A fixed repulser policy and belief models are given</li><li>Two-player Hanabi, shared reward</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Each turn, with probability lambda, imagine the partner is the repulser and flip the reward; stack 7 levels so conventions stay grounded.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s11.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CoMeDi, L-BRDiv, ROTATE, Specialists cite it as the sabotage fix; none reuses its method.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">2-player Hanabi, 4 repulsers: ~0.05-0.10 sabotages per game vs 1.1-2.4 for SPWR; both ~24/25 in self-play.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Adversarial Diversity in Hanabi</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Cui et al. · ICLR 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s12\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · LIPO 2023 · 12 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Incompatible policies give diverse cooperative partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on cross-play compatibility, MAVEN&#x27;s mutual information and MAPPO (outside); TrajeDi and Any-Play are only baseline or related work.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player cooperative game, shared reward</li><li>Several distinct solutions; simulator returns</li><li>Population size N and cross-play weight set by hand</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Each policy maximizes self-play return minus lambda times its highest cross-play return; an optional mutual-information term adds variety.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s12.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Adversity fixes its sabotage; CoMeDi adds mixed play; Specialists distills it; ROTATE modifies it; SemDiv reuses its test.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 6 recipes: recipe-mix entropy 1.58 vs 0.98 to 1.53 for baselines (Table 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Generating Diverse Cooperative Agents by Learning Incompatible Policies</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Charakorn et al. · ICLR 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s13\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · BRDiv 2023 · 13 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Make training teammates need different best responses</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Trains PLASTIC Policy (guest) as the learner on each generated set; MAA2C. Replaces TrajeDi and Any-Play diversity (both baselines). Concurrent with LIPO.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two players, full state, shared reward</li><li>Several strategies with distinct best responses</li><li>Simulator; near-optimal test teammates</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Scores a teammate set by a cross-play return matrix, high diagonal and low off-diagonal, optimized with MAA2C and a shared critic.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s13.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">L-BRDiv generalizes it; ROTATE&#x27;s regret objective subsumes it; UPD builds BRDiv evaluation partners.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Cooperative Reaching: mean return about 0.84 vs about 0.74 best baseline (notes&#x27; average); loses in Simple Cooking.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Generating Teammates for Training Robust Ad Hoc Teamwork Agents via Best-Response Diversity</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Rahman et al. · TMLR 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s14\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · E3T 2023 · 14 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: noisy self-copy replaces the partner pool</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree; here as UPD&#x27;s direct ancestor. Builds on self-play and MEP&#x27;s skilled-and-diverse idea (outside); compares against Other-Play, TrajeDi, FCP.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two players; no human data</li><li>Partner&#x27;s recent moves are visible</li><li>One end-to-end run, no pre-trained pool</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Partner = eps random + (1-eps) copy of the ego; a predictor reads the partner&#x27;s last 5 state-action pairs to guess its next action.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s14.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">UPD replaces its fixed mixture with learnability-selected partners and reuses its code; CEC compares against it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, human proxies: reward 114.1 vs 89.7 for MEP, in 10.6% of MEP&#x27;s training time (Table 2).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">An Efficient End-to-End Training Approach for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Yan et al. · NeurIPS 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s15\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · L-BRDiv 2024 · 15 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Learn the minimum coverage set of best responses</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Generalizes BRDiv&#x27;s best-response diversity (builds on): learned Lagrange weights replace fixed ones; LIPO only compared; Adversity not compared.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player cooperative Dec-POMDP, unseen teammates</li><li>Simulator rollouts; return is the only teammate cue</li><li>Size K of the estimate set before training</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Learns K best responses and K teammates together, with cross-play return constraints whose Lagrange weights are learned, not hand-set.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s15.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Cited by Specialists, SemDiv, ROTATE; no member builds on it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Full minimum coverage set found in 3 of 4 two-player games; baselines in 1 of 4 (text counts).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Minimum Coverage Sets for Training Robust Ad Hoc Teamwork Agents</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Rahman et al. · AAAI 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s16\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Specialists 2024 · 16 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Diversity is not enough: partners must be specialists</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends LIPO&#x27;s XP-min partners (its source) and applies FCP (guest) to train generalists on them; analyzes CoMeDi&#x27;s mixed play as a baseline; uses DAgger.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Hand-written play feature (the recipe completed)</li><li>A trained XP-min source population</li><li>Overcooked only; oracle generalist as ruler</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Defines diversity, specialization and overfitness; SpecTRL distills XP-min partners by RL (plus DAgger) to drop handshake sabotage.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s16.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ROTATE cites it for sabotage; no member builds on it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked: overfitness 0.54 to 0.30 with robustness 0.64 vs 0.61 for the source (small gain, Table 3).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diversity Is Not All You Need: Training A Robust Cooperative Agent Needs Specialist Partners</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Charakorn et al. · NeurIPS 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s17\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · SemDiv 2025 · 17 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">An LLM invents teammates by behavior, not policy</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on Macop&#x27;s loop and LLM reward code (outside); reuses LIPO&#x27;s cross-play test. Compares against FCP (guest) and LIPO; cites BRDiv, L-BRDiv, CoMeDi, TrajeDi.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Teammates describe their behavior in words</li><li>Simulator code an LLM can read and edit</li><li>Two-player tasks; GPT-4o and a MARL budget</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">An LLM proposes a behavior and writes reward code; a teammate trains on it; the agent keeps one policy head per teammate and an LLM picks.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s17.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">4 environments, 5 unseen teammates each: task success 0.74 vs 0.62 for best baseline Macop (+19%, Table 1).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">LLM-Assisted Semantically Diverse Teammate Generation for Efficient Multi-agent Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Li et al. · ICML 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s18\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · CEC 2025 · 18 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Many training tasks beat many training partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on IPPO self-play and JaxMARL Overcooked (outside); cites CoMeDi only as one-task partner diversity. Compares against FCP and E3T (guests): both score 0 on unseen grids.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Shared-reward tasks it can sample at will</li><li>Same dynamics; tasks differ in start state</li><li>Fast simulator with a solvable-task generator</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Self-play with one partner (itself) over ~1.16e17 procedurally generated Overcooked layouts, with no partner population.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s18.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">UPD reuses its code base and unknown-levels setting, and compares with a CEC-style baseline.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Cross-play reward ~171 on 100 unseen grids while FCP and E3T score 0 (Fig. 6; ~171 read from bars).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Cross-environment Cooperation Enables Zero-shot Multi-agent Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Jha et al. · ICML 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s19\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · ROTATE 2025 · 19 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Train the agent against teammates it can&#x27;t yet handle</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Its regret objective matches BRDiv&#x27;s and LIPO&#x27;s adversarial diversity (builds on); reuses CoMeDi&#x27;s switching data and OBL&#x27;s belief model (guest). FCP baseline.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Competent but unseen teammates</li><li>Resettable simulator, two agents</li><li>Teammate and best response trained from scratch each round</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Alternate: generate a teammate that maximizes ego regret yet stays competent (return bonus), store it, train the ego with PPO on the buffer.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s19.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">UPD reuses its code to build evaluation partners and compares with it in an appendix table.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Best on 5 of 6 tasks; Cramped Room return 175.1 vs 145.2 for CoMeDi (Table 3).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">ROTATE: Regret-driven Open-ended Training for Ad Hoc Teamwork</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Wang et al. · arXiv 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-s20\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · UPD 2026 · 20 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Partners made on the fly, kept if learnable</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on E3T&#x27;s partner mixing (guest), replacing its fixed ego-random mixture; reuses ROTATE&#x27;s code, BRDiv evaluation partners and CEC&#x27;s code base. FCP, E3T baselines.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two players, self-play against one frozen partner</li><li>Cheap parallel simulation (overhead under 10%)</li><li>Joint version needs a level generator</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Generate candidate partners (random mix, optional action bias), score each by return variance, and train the ego only on the top-scoring ones.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/e1_s20.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked-AI: average return 94.4 vs 78.8 for E3T (Table 1); OGC random levels: JUPD 58.9.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Unsupervised Partner Design Enables Robust Ad-hoc Teamwork</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Ruhdorfer et al. · ICML 2026</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Each paper wins on its own test; no shared benchmark</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Hanabi cross-play (Other-Play, OBL, Any-Play, Adversity, meta-agent), then Overcooked as the common test.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Reaching, foraging and matrix games (BRDiv, L-BRDiv) score returns against unseen heuristic teammates.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Newest papers vary tasks and add people: CEC (80), CoMeDi (25), UPD (12); FCP (114) and E3T (20) set the baselines.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#F7EEE3;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Benchmarks, partner sets and metrics differ, so never rank rows; each shows one paper&#x27;s gain over its own baselines.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">Adversity</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">2-player Hanabi, 4 repulsers</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">~0.05-0.10 sabotages per game vs 1.1-2.4 (SPWR)</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">LIPO</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked recipe-mix entropy</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">1.58 vs 0.98 to 1.53 for baselines</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">BRDiv</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Cooperative Reaching, mean return</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">about 0.84 vs about 0.74; loses Simple Cooking</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">CoMeDi</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked Coordination Ring, 25 users</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Score 3.68 vs 2.08 self-play; expert human 3.25</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">L-BRDiv</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">4 two-player games, MCS found</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Full set in 3 of 4; baselines 1 of 4</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">ROTATE</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">LBF + 5 Overcooked layouts, return</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Best on 5 of 6 tasks; Cramped Room 175.1 vs 145.2</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">CEC</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked, 100 unseen grids</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Cross-play ~171; FCP and E3T score 0</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#80511E\">UPD</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked-AI, 5 layouts</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Average return 94.4 vs 78.8 for E3T</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diverse partners and environments</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (6 guests) · 2017–2026 · OBL → LIPO → BRDiv → ROTATE</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"e1-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#80511E\">Diverse partners · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #80511E;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#80511E;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Test partners are author-built, partly with BRDiv itself (ROTATE, UPD); there is no shared evaluation set.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #80511E;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#80511E;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Sabotage and overfitting fixes are small or need extra assumptions (Adversity, CoMeDi, Specialists&#x27; +0.03).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #80511E;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#80511E;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Mostly two-player, simulated tests; larger teams and people are barely tested (CoMeDi 25, UPD 12 users).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Diverse partners and environments</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (6 guests) · 2017–2026 · OBL → LIPO → BRDiv → ROTATE</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Members (14, by year): Type-free AHT 2021, TrajeDi 2021, Any-Play 2022, Hanabi meta-agent 2023, CoMeDi 2023, Adversity 2023, LIPO 2023, BRDiv 2023, L-BRDiv 2024, Specialists 2024, SemDiv 2025, CEC 2025, ROTATE 2025, UPD 2026. Guests (6, home tree in brackets): PLASTIC 2017 [Inferring teammates], Other-Play 2020 [Symmetry and belief], OBL 2021 [Symmetry and belief], Conventions 2021 [Symmetry and belief], FCP 2021 [Population training for humans], E3T 2023 [Population training for humans]. Old trees: t3 and t1 overlapped this one. Edges relied on (inheritance, kind 0): Adversity-&gt;LIPO, L-BRDiv-&gt;BRDiv, ROTATE-&gt;LIPO, ROTATE-&gt;BRDiv, plus the guest edges Adversity-&gt;OBL and UPD-&gt;E3T. Reuse (kind 1): CoMeDi-&gt;LIPO, Specialists-&gt;LIPO, SemDiv-&gt;LIPO, ROTATE-&gt;CoMeDi, UPD-&gt;BRDiv, UPD-&gt;CEC, UPD-&gt;ROTATE, plus the guest edges OBL-&gt;Other-Play, TrajeDi-&gt;Other-Play, Adversity-&gt;Other-Play, CoMeDi-&gt;Conventions, BRDiv-&gt;PLASTIC, Specialists-&gt;FCP, ROTATE-&gt;OBL. Everything else (46 edges, including 12 involving guests) is a citation or a baseline comparison (kind 2): for example Any-Play compares against Other-Play and OBL; CEC, SemDiv, ROTATE, UPD and E3T compare against FCP; CEC compares against E3T; Type-free AHT compares against PLASTIC. What guests change: the line now reaches back to the zero-shot lane (Other-Play, OBL feed TrajeDi, Adversity, ROTATE; Conventions feeds CoMeDi), to the type-based AHT learner (PLASTIC feeds BRDiv&#x27;s evaluation), to FCP (Specialists) and forward to E3T, the direct ancestor of UPD. Honest gaps: Type-free AHT, Any-Play and Hanabi meta-agent still have no solid edge to other members (Type-free AHT and Any-Play only compare against guests). They join by topic. Figure: 9-node backbone (OBL, LIPO, Adversity, CoMeDi, BRDiv, L-BRDiv, ROTATE, E3T, UPD); CEC, Specialists, SemDiv and the early papers are omitted. Survey vocabulary: all members are training-time teammate generation (Mirsky et al. 2022: ST2 and ST4 learners); the Hanabi meta-agent and PLASTIC are type-based reasoning (Albrecht and Stone 2018); SemDiv adds online selection (ST4). Uncertain: PLASTIC and Conventions each rest on one reuse edge; the &#x27;no diversity check&#x27; remark about FCP is Any-Play&#x27;s wording of FCP&#x27;s own stated limitation.","Shared assumptions, with the paper that relaxes each: 1. Simulator for partners: all members train partners and the learner on rollouts. ROTATE needs resets to arbitrary states; Adversity needs belief models learned from true hands; SemDiv needs code an LLM can read. 2. Two agents with a shared reward: Hanabi 2-player (Any-Play, Adversity, Hanabi meta-agent, TrajeDi), Overcooked 2-player (LIPO, CoMeDi, Specialists, CEC, ROTATE, UPD), HFO two-agent teams (Type-free AHT), small games (BRDiv, L-BRDiv). ROTATE&#x27;s appendix has a 4-agent LBF run (0.887 vs 0.672) and a partially observable LBF run (0.89 vs 0.70). 3. Distinct solutions: LIPO and BRDiv assume several ways to cooperate; Specialists adds a hand-written play feature (the recipe completed); L-BRDiv needs the size K of the estimate set chosen before training. 4. Fixed partners at test time: no member tests with partners that adapt to the learner. Human studies: FCP 114 participants (guest), E3T 20 (guest), CoMeDi 25 users (Coordination Ring), CEC 80 Prolific participants, UPD 12 people. 5. Relaxations: Type-free AHT drops hand-made types (PLASTIC, a guest, assumes types and known dynamics); OBL (guest) drops Other-Play&#x27;s symmetry input but needs a belief model and a steppable simulator; E3T (guest) drops the pre-trained partner pool of FCP but needs visible partner moves; Adversity needs partial observability and public actions, and CoMeDi removes that limit with mixed play; ROTATE keeps generated teammates competent; CEC drops the partner population and varies tasks; SemDiv needs teammates that describe their behavior. Builds on / achieves: see tree_notes.tldr and per-paper notes. Numbers on different benchmarks are not comparable.","Mechanism by stage. Guests: Other-Play (random relabeled copy of the partner, given symmetries) and OBL (past as fixed pi0, future as pi1, learned belief) show how to avoid arbitrary conventions; Conventions splits the policy into a task and a partner module; FCP builds a pool of seeds and checkpoints and trains a best response; E3T replaces the pool with a noisy self-copy plus a partner predictor; PLASTIC infers the past teammate type a new team resembles. Stage 1, spread policies apart: Type-free AHT (target entropy per episode), TrajeDi (Jensen-Shannon divergence between trajectory distributions plus a common best response), Any-Play (decode a random intent), Hanabi meta-agent (MAP-Elites pool of rule-based agents plus a Bayes belief over partner type; this one also adapts at test time). Stage 2, cross-play and best response: LIPO (self-play minus lambda times highest cross-play); Adversity (coin flip per turn between repulser and self, with OBL-style belief resampling, to prevent sabotage); CoMeDi (adds mixed play, a self-play rollout after a cross-play prefix); BRDiv (cross-play return matrix over teammate and best-response pairs); L-BRDiv (learned Lagrange weights, minimum coverage set); Specialists (distill XP-min partners, define specialization and overfitness). Stage 3, regret, language, tasks: SemDiv (LLM writes behaviors and reward code, then picks a policy head); ROTATE (teammate maximizes ego regret with a competence bonus; its regret objective is the BRDiv/LIPO objective with per-state competence guarantees); CEC (no partners, many generated tasks); UPD (sample partner mixtures, keep those with high return variance, i.e. learnable ones). Edges: Adversity-&gt;LIPO, Adversity-&gt;OBL, UPD-&gt;E3T, L-BRDiv-&gt;BRDiv, ROTATE-&gt;LIPO, ROTATE-&gt;BRDiv are inheritance; CoMeDi-&gt;LIPO, CoMeDi-&gt;Conventions, Specialists-&gt;FCP, ROTATE-&gt;OBL, Adversity-&gt;Other-Play, TrajeDi-&gt;Other-Play, BRDiv-&gt;PLASTIC, OBL-&gt;Other-Play, Specialists-&gt;LIPO, SemDiv-&gt;LIPO, ROTATE-&gt;CoMeDi, UPD-&gt;BRDiv, UPD-&gt;CEC, UPD-&gt;ROTATE are reuse of an idea, code or test. The rest are citations and baselines. Specialists and SemDiv use LIPO but add steps outside the partner-diversity line (distillation, LLMs).","Mechanism by stage. Guests: Other-Play (random relabeled copy of the partner, given symmetries) and OBL (past as fixed pi0, future as pi1, learned belief) show how to avoid arbitrary conventions; Conventions splits the policy into a task and a partner module; FCP builds a pool of seeds and checkpoints and trains a best response; E3T replaces the pool with a noisy self-copy plus a partner predictor; PLASTIC infers the past teammate type a new team resembles. Stage 1, spread policies apart: Type-free AHT (target entropy per episode), TrajeDi (Jensen-Shannon divergence between trajectory distributions plus a common best response), Any-Play (decode a random intent), Hanabi meta-agent (MAP-Elites pool of rule-based agents plus a Bayes belief over partner type; this one also adapts at test time). Stage 2, cross-play and best response: LIPO (self-play minus lambda times highest cross-play); Adversity (coin flip per turn between repulser and self, with OBL-style belief resampling, to prevent sabotage); CoMeDi (adds mixed play, a self-play rollout after a cross-play prefix); BRDiv (cross-play return matrix over teammate and best-response pairs); L-BRDiv (learned Lagrange weights, minimum coverage set); Specialists (distill XP-min partners, define specialization and overfitness). Stage 3, regret, language, tasks: SemDiv (LLM writes behaviors and reward code, then picks a policy head); ROTATE (teammate maximizes ego regret with a competence bonus; its regret objective is the BRDiv/LIPO objective with per-state competence guarantees); CEC (no partners, many generated tasks); UPD (sample partner mixtures, keep those with high return variance, i.e. learnable ones). Edges: Adversity-&gt;LIPO, Adversity-&gt;OBL, UPD-&gt;E3T, L-BRDiv-&gt;BRDiv, ROTATE-&gt;LIPO, ROTATE-&gt;BRDiv are inheritance; CoMeDi-&gt;LIPO, CoMeDi-&gt;Conventions, Specialists-&gt;FCP, ROTATE-&gt;OBL, Adversity-&gt;Other-Play, TrajeDi-&gt;Other-Play, BRDiv-&gt;PLASTIC, OBL-&gt;Other-Play, Specialists-&gt;LIPO, SemDiv-&gt;LIPO, ROTATE-&gt;CoMeDi, UPD-&gt;BRDiv, UPD-&gt;CEC, UPD-&gt;ROTATE are reuse of an idea, code or test. The rest are citations and baselines. Specialists and SemDiv use LIPO but add steps outside the partner-diversity line (distillation, LLMs).","Guest from the Inferring teammates tree. Barrett, Rosenfeld, Kraus, Stone; AIJ 2017 (p3). Why here: BRDiv&#x27;s evaluation protocol trains PLASTIC Policy as the ad hoc learner on each generated teammate set (kind 1, reuse of a component). Type-free AHT compares against a hindsight upper-bound PLASTIC variant in pursuit and HFO (kind 2). Nothing in this tree inherits PLASTIC&#x27;s belief update or planning, so the guest sits at the start of the line as the type-based learner the generators feed. Method (Sec. 4): learn offline from past teammates; PLASTIC-Model learns a decision-tree model per type and plans with UCT; PLASTIC-Policy learns a Fitted Q Iteration policy per past team and follows the most likely one. Belief update uses polynomial weights (loss-bounded) so one wrong prediction does not drop a good type; uniform prior. Numbers (as in its notes): pursuit, 20x20 grid, prey caught per 500 steps, 1,000 trials. Known hand-coded teammates: GP 15.07 vs Match 6.12; PD 14.47 vs 2.60. Unseen student teams (4 hand-coded models, none true): 13.36 vs Match 9.97; the +34% is the notes&#x27; arithmetic. HFO 2 v 2: Combined policy 0.382 (text); other HFO bar values are read off Fig. 15 and are approximate (Correct policy about 0.42, PLASTIC-Policy about 0.42). Bandit selection needs about 10,000 episodes to reach 0.418 where PLASTIC-Policy needs a few. Caveats: assumes known environment dynamics and stationary teammates like past ones; whether HFO teams count as unseen is unclear, since policies were learned for all 7 types. Numbers from different papers in this tree are not comparable.","Guest from the Symmetry and belief tree. Hu, Lerer, Peysakhovich, Foerster; ICML 2020 (p36). Why here: it defines zero-shot coordination and supplies the OP agents that three members train or compare with. Kind 1 (reuse): OBL (adopts the ZSC setting; uses OP agents as baseline and partners; OBL&#x27;s own method needs no symmetries), TrajeDi (its Hanabi policies are OP plus an auxiliary task; TrajeDi is positioned as complementary to OP when symmetries are unknown), Adversity (its Rank Bot repulser is an OP policy; it adopts OP&#x27;s intra-algorithm cross-play metric). Kind 2 (compared): Any-Play (SAD+OP and SAD+AUX+OP cross-played as baselines), E3T (listed as a baseline). Method (Sec. 4-5): maximize the return of agent 1 with a random symmetry-equivalent copy of agent 2; implemented by giving each agent a random relabeling (Hanabi: color permutations) every episode, on top of SAD. Numbers (Table 1, Hanabi cross-play, 12 models per method, max 25): SAD 2.52 +/- 0.34; SAD+OP 15.32 +/- 0.65; SAD+AUX 17.65 +/- 0.69; SAD+AUX+OP 22.07 +/- 0.11. Self-play stays near 24 for all. Lever game: OP 0.9 vs self-play about 0.1 at test. Human study (20 players, preliminary per the authors): average score 15.75 with the OP bot vs 9.15 with a self-play bot. Caveats: needs the symmetries; the authors call the human results preliminary and say OP will not work in every zero-shot problem; which self-play variant the human-study bot is stays unclear. Numbers are not comparable with other papers&#x27; Hanabi or Overcooked scores.","Xing, Liu, Zheng, Pan; IJCAI 2021 (p20). Tree edges: child (kind 2) of the guest PLASTIC (a hindsight upper-bound PLASTIC variant is compared in pursuit and HFO; home: Inferring teammates tree); no inheritance edge. Parent only by citation (kind 2) of BRDiv, L-BRDiv and ROTATE, which list it among information-theoretic or entropy-based teammate generation. AATEAM is outside this tree. Method (Sec. 4.1): EDRQN maximizes reward with policy entropy equal to a target re-sampled each episode; Boltzmann policy softmax(Q/alpha); a Q-network and an alpha-network (both GRUs) take the target as input. Numbers (Table 1, HFO scoring %): unseen teams PLASTIC 70.3, AATEAM 73.2, EDRQN 77.3; prepared teams PLASTIC 73.4, AATEAM 76.5, EDRQN 75.4 (EDRQN mean computed by the notes, not printed). EDRQN leads 4 of 5 unseen and 2 of 5 prepared teams. PLASTIC and AATEAM were given the prepared teams as types; EDRQN was not. Pursuit (28x28 torus, 2 predators): EDRQN beats PLASTIC in 13 of 18 cases, loses 5; PLASTIC is a hindsight upper bound; which PLASTIC capacity the 13/5 count uses is unverified. Uncertain: the paper does not detail how the ad hoc agent is pre-trained with generated teammates; no code link; the self-play-access assumption is the notes&#x27; reading.","Guest from the Symmetry and belief tree. Hu, Lerer, Cui, Pineda, Brown, Foerster; ICML 2021 (p37). Why here: it carries the line back to the zero-shot lane. Edges: child of Other-Play (kind 1: adopts its ZSC setting, uses OP agents as baseline and partners; its own method needs no symmetries). Parent of Adversity (kind 0: Adversity builds on OBL&#x27;s fictitious transitions and belief hierarchy and adds repulsive transitions), ROTATE (kind 1: recurrent belief model for SXP/XSP trajectory collection inspired by OBL). Kind 2 parent of Any-Play (level-5 OBL agents are a cross-play baseline). Method (Sec. 4): sample the hidden state as if everyone had played pi0, apply the action, let the partner act with pi1, learn from the fictitious rewards (LB-OBL, R2D2 backbone, RNN belief model); reuse pi1 as the next pi0 for levels 2, 3, 4. With pi0 uniform random, level 1 is a grounded policy. Numbers (Table 1, 2-player Hanabi, 5 seeds, max 25; SP / XP): Other-Play 24.14 / 21.77; K-Level 16.97 / 17.17; OBL level 1 20.92 / 20.85; level 2 23.41 / 23.24; level 3 23.93 / 23.68; level 4 24.10 / 23.76. With the human-data Clone Bot: OBL 13.80 to 16.02 vs Other-Play 8.55, K-Level 13.03. 3-player: level 4 XP 23.02 vs OP 17.36 (text only). Caveats: below OP in self-play at level 1 and below OP with Rank Bot (which came from an OP run); Clone Bot is only a proxy for humans (no human study); SAD numbers are not directly comparable (different networks); Any-Play reports OBL at only 5.2 inter-algorithm cross-play, a different protocol. That LB-OBL needs a steppable simulator is the notes&#x27; reading.","Guest from the Symmetry and belief tree. Shih, Sawhney, Kondic, Ermon, Sadigh; ICLR 2021 (p39). Why here: one reuse edge, Conventions to CoMeDi (kind 1): CoMeDi trains its convention-aware agent from the diverse policy set following multitask routines shown effective in Shih et al. Shih and Sadigh are authors of both papers. Nothing else in the tree builds on it. Method (Sec. 5): policy = task module x partner module, pi_i(a|s) proportional to g_t(a|s) g_p_i(a|z); regularizer pulls the task module toward the average response over partners (weight lambda 0 to 0.5); PPO. New partner: keep the task module, learn a new partner module. New task: fine-tune the task module, keep partner modules frozen. Numbers (Fig. 7 labels, Wasserstein distance of the task module to oracle marginals, lower is better; lambda 0.5 / 0.3 / 0.0 / BaselineAgg / EarlyStopping): Arms 3: 0.01 / 0.01 / 0.46 / 1.15 / 0.75; Blocks: 0.10 / 0.15 / 0.53 / 0.44 / 0.46. Adaptation and zero-shot curves (Figs. 6, 9, 10) print no values. Caveats: the 0.01 vs 1.15 headline is Arms 3 only; the lambda 0.5 label in Arms 2 is paired by legend order; the paper says it is unclear whether a low-dimensional latent helps. The edge to CoMeDi rests on a single sentence in CoMeDi and could be read as a citation (kind 2).","Lupu et al., ICML 2021 (p44). Tree edges: child of the guest Other-Play (kind 1: its Hanabi policies are trained with Other-Play plus an auxiliary task; the paper positions TrajeDi as complementary to OP when symmetries are unknown). It is also a kind 2 baseline of the guest E3T (human-proxy results reused from the MEP paper). Cited only (kind 2) by CoMeDi, Any-Play, Adversity, L-BRDiv, Specialists, ROTATE; reimplemented as a baseline by LIPO and BRDiv; a Table 1 baseline in SemDiv (SemDiv&#x27;s edge to TrajeDi is a citation). Numbers, Table 1 (Hanabi 2p; SP / XP): Other-Play agents (5) 24.24 +/- 0.02 / 23.65 +/- 0.06; BR to plain pool (4 BRs) 24.17 +/- 0.04 / 23.66 +/- 0.07; BR to TrajeDi pool 24.22 +/- 0.01 / 24.09 +/- 0.02. Intervals are one standard error. The XP gain over Other-Play is 0.44 points on a scale of about 24. Tree MDP: even with entropic init the plain pool puts over 85% on one leaf; TrajeDi finds all optima. Method: Eq. 2/7 loss = rewards of every pool member and the BR plus alpha times TrajeDi (generalized JSD over trajectory distributions, with action discounting gamma). All Hanabi agents use Other-Play plus an auxiliary task and the last action is hidden, which the paper admits changes the game. Why later papers move on (their words): LIPO notes that locally different decisions need not give different high-level behavior; L-BRDiv says trajectory diversity is not necessarily meaningful for teammates; Any-Play calls TrajeDi more complicated and says it needed a modified Hanabi. Caveats: appendix derivations not read; few seeds (4 BRs, 5 agents; compute-limited per the paper); no statistical test beyond standard errors; the method diagram on its slides is my own sketch.","Guest from the Population training for humans tree. Strouse, McKee, Botvinick, Hughes, Everett; NeurIPS 2021 (p61). Why here: the plain seed-and-checkpoint pool is what the diversity papers set out to beat, and Specialists actually uses it. Kind 1: Specialists (always applies FCP, past-checkpoint partners, when training generalists on the generated populations). Kind 2 (baseline or discussion): Any-Play (discussed as a similar diversity method; not run, no Hanabi FCP agents), SemDiv (reimplemented as two-stage PBT baseline, Table 1), CEC (single-task baseline in Overcooked and Dual Destination), ROTATE (teammate-generation baseline), UPD (Table 1 baseline), E3T (baseline vs E3T on human proxy, AI partners, humans). Method: stage 1 trains N = 32 self-play agents differing in seed, saved at initialization, 50% of final reward and the end; stage 2 trains the agent (V-MPO, ResNet+LSTM) as the best response to the frozen pool. Numbers (Table 1, deliveries, 5 seeds, averaged over 5 layouts; FCP / FCP-T): human proxy 10.6 / 4.7; diverse SP 11.2 / 6.9; random 8.6 / 1.0. SP, PP, BCP baselines appear only as bars (Fig. 5, 7a); the paper prints no numbers for them. Human study: 114 participants, FCP best on deliveries (p &lt; 0.001 vs SP, PP, BCP, FCP-T) and preferred over every other agent (p &lt; 0.05). Caveats: FCP-T is an ablation (final checkpoints only), not a baseline. Other papers&#x27; FCP numbers (E3T Table 2: 71.7; UPD Table 1: 70.0; SemDiv Table 1: 0.48 task success) come from their own reimplementations and setups and are not comparable with each other or with this paper. The 32 x 3 pool size is the notes&#x27; arithmetic.","Lucas and Allen; AAMAS 2022 (p45). Tree edges: no kind 0 or 1 edge. Kind 2 children of the guests Other-Play (SAD+OP and SAD+AUX+OP cross-played as baselines), OBL (level-5 OBL agents as baseline) and FCP (discussed as a similar diversity method; not run, no Hanabi FCP agents). Other kind 2: child of Hanabi meta-agent (cited as expert-designed rule-based pool that does not generalize) and TrajeDi (concurrent work, discussed; no quantitative comparison). Parent (kind 2) of LIPO (related-work citation), BRDiv (reimplemented baseline), Specialists, ROTATE. Method (Sec. 3.2): R = R_env + lambda x log q(z | o). Intent z drawn uniformly from N options each episode; specializer gets it one-hot; accommodator&#x27;s LSTM plus a head predicts z; only the accommodator is used at test time. Applied to SAD and SAD+AUX. Numbers (Table 1, Hanabi score out of 25, inter-XP): SAD+AUX+AP 14.2; SAD+AUX 13.1; SAD+AUX+OP 13.1; SAD+OP 12.2; IQL 11.1; SAD+AP 10.3; VDN 9.2; SAD 7.3; OBL 5.2. OBL leads self-play and same-algorithm play (24.2) but scores 5.2 inter-XP. Any-Play has the lowest self-play scores but the highest inter-XP. Caveats: margin 14.2 vs 13.1 is small; two baselines tie at 13.1; the pool is mostly VDN/SAD-based; only 2-player Hanabi with AI teammates; no human test; no comparison with TrajeDi or Fictitious Co-Play. Code: github.com/mit-ll/hanabi_AnyPlay (not checked).","Canaan, Gao, Togelius, Nealen, Menzel; listed as IEEE ToG 2023 (p24). The PDF is arXiv v3 (Aug 2022) and names no journal; Specialists cites it as IEEE Trans. Games 15(2), 2022. Tree edges: no parent. Kind 2 parent of Any-Play (rule-based pools do not generalize to learned agents), LIPO (quality-diversity needs domain knowledge) and Specialists (QD with domain knowledge). Method: MAP-Elites evolves rule-based agents (15 integers choosing among 135 rules) into a 5x5 map by Communicativeness and IPP; coverage 22 of 25 niches; max self-play 19.54 / 19.95 / 20.00 over three runs. Offline: 400 games per (response, partner type) pair. Online: uniform belief, Bayes update at the start of each game from the two features (normal likelihood, std 0.1), play the response with the best belief-weighted score. In survey terms this is type-based reasoning (Albrecht and Stone) with pool generation. Numbers (Table IV, average score of 2000 games, same-pool cells): Pop 1 Oracle 13.42, Generalist 12.94, Adaptive 13.16; Pop 2 12.80 / 11.90 / 12.23; Pop 3 14.00 / 13.91 / 13.99. Random Response 8.02 to 9.58 over all cells. Gains of Adaptive over Generalist: +0.22, +0.33, +0.08; pop 1 and 2 differ at p &lt; 0.0002, pop 3 not significant. Caveats: on unseen pools Generalist wins by about 0.2 in two cells (inconclusive to slightly negative); no humans or non-rule-based partners; Generalist and Random Response are the authors&#x27; own baselines; venue taken from the file name.","Sarkar, Shih, Sadigh, NeurIPS 2023 (p42). Edges: child of the guest Conventions (kind 1: the convention-aware agent is trained from the diverse policy set following multitask routines shown effective in Shih et al.; shared authors Shih and Sadigh; home: Symmetry and belief tree; one sentence supports the edge). Child of LIPO (kind 1: its pure cross-play minimization baseline is LIPO&#x27;s idea, which CoMeDi extends with mixed play); child of TrajeDi (kind 2, cited as statistical diversity) and Adversity (kind 2, concurrent work on handshakes, but ADVERSITY cannot work in a fully observable game). Parent of ROTATE (kind 1: reuses policy-switching data collection, relates objective to mixed play); kind 2 parent of Specialists (own reimplementation as the MP-reg baseline), SemDiv (cited), CEC (cited). Numbers: Coordination Ring user study (Fig. 7, 25 Prolific users, 40-second games): CoMeDi 3.68, XP 2.48, ADAP 2.24, SP 2.08; expert human partner 3.25 (p = 0.045; separate 8 in-person users). Cramped Room (App. B.10): CoMeDi 4.72, XP 4.28, SP 3.68, ADAP 3.36. Balance Beam (Table 1): handshake games 0.16 at beta = 0, 0.00 at beta = 0.5. Method: loss -J(pi_n, pi_n) + alpha J(pi_n, pi*) - beta J_M(pi_n, pi*); pi* is the most compatible earlier convention; built on MAPPO; pool distilled into one convention-aware agent by behavior cloning plus RL. Caveats: Fig. 7 axis says Dishes Served, text says score (treated as the same); the XP baseline is beta = 0, not LIPO itself; small study; inconsistent results with unseen partners on two simulated layouts (App. B.12); Specialists reports that mixed play can over-correct and reduce specialization; code only mentioned on the authors&#x27; website, not checked.","Cui, Lupu, Sokota, Hu, Wu, Foerster; ICLR 2023 (p46; short name Adversity). Edges: child of the guest OBL (kind 0: builds on OBL&#x27;s fictitious transitions and belief hierarchy, adding repulsive transitions to train diverse adversaries; home: Symmetry and belief tree) and of the guest Other-Play (kind 1: its Rank Bot repulser is an Other-Play policy and it adopts OP&#x27;s intra-algorithm cross-play metric). Child of LIPO (kind 0: closest work; the problem and low-cross-play idea come from LIPO, and Adversity adds per-turn partner resampling via off-belief learning; LIPO&#x27;s appendix calls it an extension of LIPO) and TrajeDi (kind 2, cited as a diversity loss with arbitrary conventions). Parent (kind 2) of CoMeDi, L-BRDiv (not compared, since L-BRDiv keeps sabotaging policies), Specialists, ROTATE (cited for sabotage); LIPO cites it as concurrent. Method (Sec. 5, Alg. 1): with probability lambda use the repulser&#x27;s belief model, repulser as partner, reward weight -1; otherwise vanilla OBL. Resampling at every history stops the adversary from learning to detect the repulser. 7 levels, lambda 0.25 falling by 0.08 per level to 0; PPO replaces recurrent Q-learning. Needs the simulator and belief models. Numbers: Table 2 (sabotages per game with its own repulser, 3 seeds): Adversity Rank Bot 0.06, Color Bot 0.10, Clone Bot 0.05, OBL 0.09; SPWR 2.36, 1.14, 2.17, 2.04. Table 1: self-play 23.94 to 24.22; cross-play with the repulser 1.94 / 2.98 / 7.48 / 9.07 (SPWR about 0). Seed-to-seed cross-play (intra-AXP) of Adversity vs SPWR: Rank Bot 24.1 vs 0.6, Clone Bot 21.4 vs 6.2; lower for Color Bot and OBL. Caveats: Hanabi only, 2 players; high compute, hard to scale to many adversaries (Sec. 8); the paper gives no formal definition of diversity; code repo footnote not checked; the 1.1 to 2.4 range is SPWR over four repulsers.","Charakorn, Manoonpong, Dilokthanakul; ICLR 2023 (p47). Edges: no member as parent except kind 2 citations (Hanabi meta-agent: QD needs domain knowledge; TrajeDi: baseline; Any-Play: related MI objective; Adversity: concurrent). Child-of-LIPO edges: Adversity (kind 0), ROTATE (kind 0: its regret objective corresponds to LIPO&#x27;s adversarial diversity, modified to keep teammates competent), CoMeDi (kind 1), Specialists (kind 1: uses LIPO&#x27;s cross-play-minimization, with mutual information, populations as the source it distills), SemDiv (kind 1: reuses LIPO&#x27;s cross-play return-drop test for distinctness; also a Table 1 baseline). L-BRDiv compares against it (kind 2). Method (Sec. 3): J_LIPO = J_SP - lambda_XP x max over other policies of J_XP. Max works better than average (authors&#x27; guess: conflicting gradients). Optional MI term with lambda_MI = 0.5 in Overcooked. Learner: MAPPO. Numbers (Table 1, entropy of recipe mix, 5 seeds): LIPO 1.58, Multi MAVEN 1.53, Multi SPMI 1.43, Multi SP 1.16, TrajeDi 0.98, LIPO without MI 1.26. Standard errors overlap for LIPO vs Multi MAVEN, so the top is not cleanly separated. Generalist trained on LIPO partners: harmonic mean 0.60 vs 0.37 to 0.51 against specialists; 0.82 vs 0.72 to 0.75 on held-out populations (Fig. 9). That LIPO finds all 32 CMG solutions at population 32 is read off Fig. 3 (approximate). Caveats (App. F, G): LIPO agents can act adversarially to other partners; Adversity later shows they sabotage; extra hyperparameter; tested only on discrete multi-solution tasks. Code: 51616/marl-lipo (not checked).","Rahman, Fosong, Carlucho, Albrecht; TMLR 2023 (p49). Edges: child of Type-free AHT (kind 2, cited), TrajeDi and Any-Play (kind 2, reimplemented baselines); Child of the guest PLASTIC (kind 1: its evaluation protocol uses PLASTIC Policy as the ad hoc learner trained on each generated teammate set; home: Inferring teammates tree). Parent of L-BRDiv (kind 0: generalizes the objective with learned Lagrange multipliers and the minimum coverage set), ROTATE (kind 0: per-trajectory regret subsumes BRDiv&#x27;s adversarial diversity), UPD (kind 1: BRDiv agents built as evaluation partners); kind 2 parent of Specialists, SemDiv. Concurrent with LIPO; no edge. Method: K teammates each with a best-response partner; K x K cross-play matrix C; BRDiv = trace(C) + sum_{i!=j}(C_ii - C_ij) + sum_{i!=j}(C_ii - C_ji). MAA2C with a shared critic. Generation uses 16M timesteps (K=4, Cooperative Reaching), 200M (K=6 LBF, K=8 Simple Cooking), 5 seeds. Numbers: the paper prints NO overall mean. Slide values are the notes&#x27; own unweighted averages of per-heuristic cells read from Fig. 5: Cooperative Reaching BRDiv about 0.84 vs TrajeDi075 about 0.74 (Independent about 0.55); LBF about 0.71 vs Any-Play about 0.63; Simple Cooking BRDiv about 0.28 vs Any-Play about 0.50. Second decimals may be misread. The paper states BRDiv significantly outperforms all baselines in Cooperative Reaching and LBF with lower seed variance; in Simple Cooking against heuristic teammates it gets lower returns, because its teammates are near-optimal (17 to 20 steps) while better-scoring baselines include weak teammates. Limitation (authors): produces near-optimal teammates, not low-skill ones. Code: github.com/uoe-agents/BRDiv (not checked).","Guest from the Population training for humans tree. Yan et al.; NeurIPS 2023 (p65). Why here: it is the partner generator UPD builds on. Edges: parent of UPD (kind 0: UPD replaces E3T&#x27;s fixed ego-random mixture with learnability-selected partners; E3T is also a UPD baseline and its code is reused). Kind 2 parent of CEC (state-of-the-art single-task baseline in Overcooked, also trained within CEC). Child (kind 2, baselines it lists) of Other-Play, TrajeDi (human-proxy results reused from the MEP paper) and FCP. Method (Sec. 4): partner policy = eps x uniform random + (1-eps) x parameter copy of the ego; an encoder over the partner&#x27;s last 5 state-action pairs predicts its next action (cross-entropy); the ego sees the state plus the predicted action and trains with PPO; asymmetric input breaks conventions. Numbers (Table 2, average over 5 Overcooked layouts with human proxies, 5 seeds; hours for Cramped Room): SP 65.8 (0.5 h); PBT 66.8 (2.7 h); FCP 71.7 (7.6 h); MEP 89.7 (17.9 h); E3T 114.1 (1.9 h). 10.6% = 1.9/17.9. With 20 real humans E3T scored highest of the baselines (bar chart only, no exact numbers). Caveats: GPU named only for MEP (over 15 h); the Table 2 hours are for one layout. UPD&#x27;s own Table 1 gives E3T 78.8 and FCP 70.0 under its own setup, so E3T numbers from the two papers must not be compared. Propositions&#x27; proofs not checked.","Rahman, Cui, Stone; AAAI 2024 (p7). Edges: child of BRDiv (kind 0), LIPO (kind 2: compared in experiments, same adversarial-diversity family), TrajeDi (kind 2: not a baseline), Type-free AHT (kind 2: cited), Adversity (kind 2: not compared, because L-BRDiv keeps self-sabotaging policies to stay general). Kind 2 parent of Specialists, SemDiv, ROTATE (all citations). Method: minimum coverage set (MCS) = smallest set of best responses covering all teammates. Maximize summed self-play return subject to constraints (Eq. 10, 11) that each agent policy does better with its own partner than with others, with tolerance tau; Lagrange dual solved with MAPPO for policies and SGD for multipliers; final agent trained with RL2. Numbers (text of Sec. 6.4 and 6.5, no results table): matrix game MCS 3: L-BRDiv 3 of 3, baselines 2 of 3; Cooperative Reaching 4: all find 4; Weighted Cooperative Reaching 4: only L-BRDiv finds all (baseline count not stated); Level-based Foraging 6: L-BRDiv finds 4 or 5, baselines fewer (not stated). So &#x27;3 of 4 games&#x27; counts matrix, Cooperative Reaching and Weighted Cooperative Reaching; baselines reach the full set only in Cooperative Reaching. Returns appear only in Fig. 4 (by eye: matrix about 6.1 vs 5.85; Weighted Reach about 7.0 vs 5.3). BRDiv is marginally higher in Cooperative Reaching. Uncertain: how K is chosen; baseline MCS counts in two games. Code: raharrasy/L-BRDiv (not opened).","Charakorn, Manoonpong, Dilokthanakul; NeurIPS 2024 (p52). Edges: FCP to Specialists (kind 1: the guest FCP, past-checkpoint partners, is always applied when training generalists on the generated populations; home: Population training for humans tree). LIPO to Specialists (kind 1: LIPO&#x27;s cross-play-minimization, with mutual information, populations are the source that specialization transfer distills). Kind 2 citations: CoMeDi (own implementation as MP-reg baseline; the paper hypothesizes mixed play loses specialization and tests it), Adversity (handshakes), BRDiv and L-BRDiv (XP-min with best-response partners), TrajeDi, Any-Play, Hanabi meta-agent (related work). Same first author as LIPO. Claim: besides diversity D(P)=H(X), a partner population needs specialization S(P) = -H(X|Pi) and low overfitness O(P) = 1 - R(oracle generalist; P). XP-min partners are specialized but learn arbitrary handshakes and sabotage others. Method: SpecTRL trains a fresh partner to maximize return with its XP-min source; SpecTRL DAgger also imitates the source&#x27;s actions. Numbers (Table 3, mean of 3 seeds, robustness R against 6 unseen test specialists, with FCP): self-play 0.18; XP-min 0.56 (overfitness 0.51); XP-min + MP-reg 0.44; XP-min + MI 0.61 (overfitness 0.54); XP-min + SpecTRL DAgger 0.62 (0.29); [XP-min + MI] + SpecTRL DAgger 0.64 (0.30). Only the last row is marked as a significant robustness gain (+0.03); other SpecTRL rows show no significant R change but much lower overfitness. The abstract wording is stronger than the table. Control (Table 1): equal diversity 1.7 to 1.8; specialists R 0.81, overfit 0.73, unspecialized 0.49. Tables 1 and 2 differ slightly (0.81 vs 0.82). Limits: one domain; hand-written feature; needs a source population. Code: not checked.","Li, Yuan, Liu, Jiang, Yu; ICML 2025 (p54). Edges: FCP to SemDiv is kind 2 (the guest FCP is reimplemented as a two-stage PBT baseline in Table 1: 0.48 task success). LIPO to SemDiv is kind 1: SemDiv &#x27;follows&#x27; LIPO&#x27;s check that a new teammate lowers the agent&#x27;s return relative to earlier teammates (Eq. 2); LIPO is also a Table 1 baseline. TrajeDi, CoMeDi, BRDiv, L-BRDiv are kind 2 (cited as policy-level diversity; BRDiv and L-BRDiv as diversity by reduced compatibility). Its main inheritance is from Macop, multi-head continual learning and LLM reward code (Text2Reward, Eureka), all outside the tree. Method: LLM proposes a behavior in words given earlier behaviors and similarity feedback; a second LLM writes reward code; a teammate is trained with task reward plus decaying generated reward and verified (reward works, task done, behavior shown, novel); the agent adds a policy head per teammate with old heads frozen; at test an LLM reads the teammate&#x27;s description and picks a head. Numbers (Table 1, average over LBF, PP, SMACv2, GRF, R1 task success | R2 behavior success): SemDiv 0.74 | 0.68; Macop-R1 0.62 | 0.48; Macop-PBT 0.60 | 0.46; MEP 0.58 | 0.42; LIPO 0.55 | 0.39; LLM-Agent 0.52 | 0.47; FCP 0.48 | 0.34; Oracle 0.95 | 0.95. +19% is the relative gain over Macop (0.74/0.62, notes&#x27; arithmetic). Macop&#x27;s numbers use best-case head choice, optimistic for the baseline. SemDiv is below Oracle in all environments. Caveats: needs teammates to state their behavior; per-teammate MARL runs; the all-two-player claim is the notes&#x27; reading of Appendix D.1; Fig. 5 values not checked. Code: github.com/lilh76/SemDiv (not opened).","Jha et al., ICML 2025 (p55). Edges: child of CoMeDi (kind 2: cited among partner-diversity work limited to one environment); child (kind 2, baselines) of the guests FCP (single-task baseline in Overcooked and Dual Destination) and E3T (state-of-the-art single-task baseline, also trained within CEC). Parent of UPD (kind 1: UPD builds FCP and E3T on CEC&#x27;s published code, uses its &#x27;AHT in unknown levels&#x27; setting, and compares a CEC-inspired baseline, kind 2 in Table 2). Its own tags suggest it fits a zero-shot-coordination lane better: it diversifies environments, not partners. Numbers (Fig. 6; 6 seeds; 3B steps each; XP reward): printed in text: CEC 130.51 on the 5 original layouts, CEC-FT 41.73 on 100 unseen grids, FCP and E3T 0 on unseen grids. Read from bars (about +/-3, marked with tilde): IPPO ~102, E3T ~80, FCP ~110, CEC-FT ~164 on originals; CEC ~171 on unseen grids. CEC beats baselines on procedural generalization, p &lt; 0.0001. Human study: 80 Prolific participants, 40 per layout; CEC rated best on 7 measures with fewest collisions, but E3T still scores higher; CEC beats FCP on score (p &lt; 0.001). Caveats: fine-tuning helps on originals but hurts generality; combining CEC with E3T did not help (28.21 vs 130.51); the &#x27;why&#x27; (low-level sequences vs task structure) is the authors&#x27; hypothesis (A.10); CEC had not converged after 3B steps.","Wang, Rahman et al., arXiv 2025 (p9; PDF says only &#x27;Preprint&#x27;, v4 dated Sep 2026). Edges: child of LIPO (kind 0: regret objective corresponds to LIPO&#x27;s adversarial diversity, modified to keep teammates competent), BRDiv (kind 0: per-trajectory regret subsumes BRDiv&#x27;s adversarial diversity; its teammate generator trains with a best-response partner as in BRDiv), CoMeDi (kind 1: reuses policy-switching data collection and relates its objective to mixed play). Child of the guest OBL (kind 1: the recurrent belief model used to collect SXP/XSP trajectories is inspired by Off-Belief Learning; home: Symmetry and belief tree). Kind 2: the guest FCP is a teammate-generation baseline. Kind 2 citations: Type-free AHT (entropy), TrajeDi, Any-Play, L-BRDiv, Specialists, Adversity (sabotage). Baselines: FCP, BRDiv, CoMeDi, COLE, PAIRED, Minimax Return. Parent (kind 1) of UPD. Numbers, Table 3 (unnormalized mean return, 6 seeds), ROTATE vs best baseline: LBF 89.5 vs 85.8 (CoMeDi); Cramped Room 175.1 vs 145.2 (CoMeDi); Asymmetric Adv. 251.7 vs 258.6 (Minimax Return, ROTATE loses); Counter Circuit 83.2 vs 75.3 (PAIRED); Coordination Ring 115.5 vs 111.6 (CoMeDi); Forced Coordination 107.0 vs 103.5 (CoMeDi). Primary metric is normalized return (Fig. 3a, no printed values). Sabotage toy game: probability 0.0007 for ROTATE vs 0.4666 for per-trajectory regret. Extensions: partially observable LBF 0.89 vs 0.70 (CoMeDi); 4-agent LBF 0.887 vs 0.672. Budgets 240M-440M steps per task. Caveats: evaluation teammates built by the authors (IPPO, BRDiv, heuristics); baselines reimplemented in JAX; BRDiv trained with only 3-4 agents because larger populations degenerated; per-task best-baseline labels read from Table 3; the paper names CoMeDi as strongest baseline.","Ruhdorfer et al., ICML 2026 (p56). Edges: E3T (guest, kind 0: replaces E3T&#x27;s fixed ego-random partner mixture with learnability-selected partners; E3T is also a baseline and its code is reused; home: Population training for humans tree); FCP (guest, kind 2: Table 1 baseline). Kind 1, reuse: ROTATE (code and evaluation-partner generation; also an appendix baseline), BRDiv (BRDiv agents form part of the evaluation partners, with hardcoded and planning agents), CEC (code base for FCP, E3T and UPD; the unknown-levels setting; a CEC-inspired baseline in the OGC comparison). Its method inherits from E3T partner mixing (a guest) and SFL learnability (UED, outside the tree). Nothing builds on it. Numbers, Table 1 (Overcooked-AI average, 6 seeds): SP 41.4, FCP 70.0, MEP 75.3, E3T 78.8, UPD 94.4 +/- 2.7 (+19.8%); without bias 87.9, without learnability 90.8. UPD is below some baselines on Cramped Room. Table 2 (OGC 5x5): CEC 23.9, SFLE3T 44.0, DR-DR 49.9, JUPD 58.9. Appendix Table 3 (3 seeds): ROTATE 91.7, UPD 94.4, UPD tuned per layout 102.3. Human study: 12 people (144 games) rated UPD more adaptive, human-like and less frustrating. Uncertain: Table 1 vs Table 3 give different std (2.7 vs 2.3) and gain (+19.8% vs +18.0%) for the same mean; SFL ratio rho not stated in the main text; observability not stated in general; an earlier version had baseline configuration issues (App. B); the authors say the takeaway is competitiveness with one configuration, not dominance.","Benchmarks per member: Type-free AHT: HFO and pursuit; TrajeDi: Hanabi 2p, tree MDPs; Any-Play: Hanabi 2p inter-XP; Hanabi meta-agent: Hanabi 2p, three MAP-Elites pools; CoMeDi: Blind Bandits, Balance Beam, Overcooked user study; Adversity: Hanabi 2p, 4 repulsers; LIPO: CMG, point-mass rendezvous, Overcooked; BRDiv: Cooperative Reaching, LBF, Simple Cooking; L-BRDiv: 4 two-player games; Specialists: multi-recipe Overcooked; SemDiv: LBF, PP, SMACv2, GRF; CEC: Overcooked with 100 generated grids; ROTATE: LBF and 5 Overcooked layouts; UPD: LBF, Overcooked-AI, OGC, human study. Headline numbers as written in each paper&#x27;s notes: Type-free AHT 77.3% vs 73.2%; TrajeDi 24.09 vs 23.65; Any-Play 14.2 vs 13.1; Hanabi meta-agent 12.23 vs 11.90 (of 25, Pop. 2); CoMeDi 3.68 vs 2.08 (expert 3.25); Adversity ~0.05-0.10 vs 1.1-2.4 sabotages per game; LIPO entropy 1.58 vs 0.98-1.53; BRDiv about 0.84 vs about 0.74 (loses Simple Cooking); L-BRDiv full set in 3 of 4 games; Specialists overfitness 0.54 to 0.30, robustness 0.64 vs 0.61; SemDiv 0.74 vs 0.62; CEC ~171 vs 0; ROTATE 175.1 vs 145.2 (Cramped Room); UPD 94.4 vs 78.8. Why they are not comparable: different games, metrics (entropy, sabotage counts, returns, success rates), partner sets and baselines. Test partners in ROTATE and UPD are built partly with BRDiv. Approximate values are marked with a tilde (BRDiv, CEC). Open limits stated in the papers: Adversity is expensive and Hanabi-only; BRDiv produces near-optimal, not weak, teammates; Specialists needs a hand-written feature and gains little robustness (+0.03); UPD says its takeaway is competitiveness, not dominance; CEC&#x27;s explanation is a hypothesis. Guests&#x27; own headlines (as in their notes; not comparable): PLASTIC pursuit 13.36 vs 9.97 prey caught; Other-Play Hanabi cross-play 2.52 to 15.32; OBL Hanabi cross-play 23.76 (level 4) vs 21.77 Other-Play; Conventions 0.01 vs 1.15 distance to true marginals (Arms 3); FCP Overcooked 10.6 vs 4.7 deliveries (FCP vs FCP-T ablation, human proxy; 114 humans); E3T 114.1 vs 89.7 reward (MEP) in 10.6% of the time. UPD&#x27;s Table 1 (E3T 78.8, FCP 70.0) and E3T&#x27;s Table 2 (FCP 71.7) use different setups.","Benchmarks per member: Type-free AHT: HFO and pursuit; TrajeDi: Hanabi 2p, tree MDPs; Any-Play: Hanabi 2p inter-XP; Hanabi meta-agent: Hanabi 2p, three MAP-Elites pools; CoMeDi: Blind Bandits, Balance Beam, Overcooked user study; Adversity: Hanabi 2p, 4 repulsers; LIPO: CMG, point-mass rendezvous, Overcooked; BRDiv: Cooperative Reaching, LBF, Simple Cooking; L-BRDiv: 4 two-player games; Specialists: multi-recipe Overcooked; SemDiv: LBF, PP, SMACv2, GRF; CEC: Overcooked with 100 generated grids; ROTATE: LBF and 5 Overcooked layouts; UPD: LBF, Overcooked-AI, OGC, human study. Headline numbers as written in each paper&#x27;s notes: Type-free AHT 77.3% vs 73.2%; TrajeDi 24.09 vs 23.65; Any-Play 14.2 vs 13.1; Hanabi meta-agent 12.23 vs 11.90 (of 25, Pop. 2); CoMeDi 3.68 vs 2.08 (expert 3.25); Adversity ~0.05-0.10 vs 1.1-2.4 sabotages per game; LIPO entropy 1.58 vs 0.98-1.53; BRDiv about 0.84 vs about 0.74 (loses Simple Cooking); L-BRDiv full set in 3 of 4 games; Specialists overfitness 0.54 to 0.30, robustness 0.64 vs 0.61; SemDiv 0.74 vs 0.62; CEC ~171 vs 0; ROTATE 175.1 vs 145.2 (Cramped Room); UPD 94.4 vs 78.8. Why they are not comparable: different games, metrics (entropy, sabotage counts, returns, success rates), partner sets and baselines. Test partners in ROTATE and UPD are built partly with BRDiv. Approximate values are marked with a tilde (BRDiv, CEC). Open limits stated in the papers: Adversity is expensive and Hanabi-only; BRDiv produces near-optimal, not weak, teammates; Specialists needs a hand-written feature and gains little robustness (+0.03); UPD says its takeaway is competitiveness, not dominance; CEC&#x27;s explanation is a hypothesis. Guests&#x27; own headlines (as in their notes; not comparable): PLASTIC pursuit 13.36 vs 9.97 prey caught; Other-Play Hanabi cross-play 2.52 to 15.32; OBL Hanabi cross-play 23.76 (level 4) vs 21.77 Other-Play; Conventions 0.01 vs 1.15 distance to true marginals (Arms 3); FCP Overcooked 10.6 vs 4.7 deliveries (FCP vs FCP-T ablation, human proxy; 114 humans); E3T 114.1 vs 89.7 reward (MEP) in 10.6% of the time. UPD&#x27;s Table 1 (E3T 78.8, FCP 70.0) and E3T&#x27;s Table 2 (FCP 71.7) use different setups."]});
